],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The -ing form needs been for a perfect continuous, which is not requested.","B":"In the standard finite negative, not follows the auxiliary.","C":"Place **not after had** and keep **kept**: they had not kept.","D":"Perfect negatives do not add did; not follows the existing auxiliary."}}},{"id":"7933c77c-b99c-54ed-a47d-0bfe96bf97a2","topicSlug":"past-perfect","level":"B1","prompt":"Begin this past-perfect question: ____ they kept the meeting records before the inspection began?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Has"},{"id":"B","text":"Had"},{"id":"C","text":"Did"},{"id":"D","text":"Are"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The first auxiliary moves before the subject; do not add a new auxiliary.","explanation":"Move **had** before they. The participle kept remains after the subject.","keywords":["perfect-question"],"skill":"perfect-question","contextKey":"past-perfect-49","fingerprint":"a1fc30712ade654325d9aed7875addb8f2bc1b4895a6b60ce8073766697f5e0a","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The first auxiliary moves before the subject; do not add a new auxiliary.","Move **had** before they. The participle kept remains after the subject.","Choose **Had**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Has does not agree with they, and cannot form a past-perfect question.","B":"Move **had** before they. The participle kept remains after the subject.","C":"Did would require a base verb and would not make this perfect question.","D":"Are would create a different structure, not the requested perfect."}}},{"id":"9e2b12aa-9738-583f-a4ad-47afacef4a9c","topicSlug":"past-perfect","level":"B1","prompt":"Choose the participle for the past perfect: We had ____ the meeting records before the inspection began. (keep)","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to keep"},{"id":"B","text":"been keeping"},{"id":"C","text":"kept"},{"id":"D","text":"keeping"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Identify the past participle, rather than choosing a word only because it looks past.","explanation":"The past participle of **keep** is **kept**. It follows had in the requested perfect construction.","keywords":["perfect-participle"],"skill":"perfect-participle","contextKey":"past-perfect-49","fingerprint":"12ffae7a7403428263c56d61daa98d5c8ac21736959b158df97a5f83e2214e37","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Identify the past participle, rather than choosing a word only because it looks past.","The past participle of **keep** is **kept**. It follows had in the requested perfect construction.","Choose **kept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is the to-infinitive, not a past participle.","B":"This would make a perfect continuous construction, not perfect simple.","C":"The past participle of **keep** is **kept**. It follows had in the requested perfect construction.","D":"This is the -ing form, not the past participle."}}},{"id":"5ec87eb4-30b4-5ba6-afa8-90ec3c8e63e5","topicSlug":"past-perfect","level":"B1","prompt":"Use the past perfect: She ____ the kitchen floor before the inspection began.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"had sweeping"},{"id":"B","text":"had to sweep"},{"id":"C","text":"had been sweeping"},{"id":"D","text":"had swept"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Find the past reference and use **have + past participle**, with the auxiliary in the appropriate tense.","explanation":"The requested past perfect needs **had + swept** with she. The reference is before the inspection began.","keywords":["perfect-affirmative"],"skill":"perfect-affirmative","contextKey":"past-perfect-50","fingerprint":"8ea9add23ae81f0e3411599e4368bd80dbf8293961f89a9da2f6ea954d1baac2","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Find the past reference and use **have + past participle**, with the auxiliary in the appropriate tense.","The requested past perfect needs **had + swept** with she. The reference is before the inspection began.","Choose **had swept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The perfect simple takes a past participle, not an -ing form.","B":"An infinitive cannot follow the perfect auxiliary here.","C":"This is a perfect continuous form; the instruction specifically asks for perfect simple.","D":"The requested past perfect needs **had + swept** with she. The reference is before the inspection began."}}},{"id":"cc68b641-0ad1-5bf2-a900-44c7cea1043e","topicSlug":"past-perfect","level":"B1","prompt":"Make a past-perfect negative: They ____ the kitchen floor before the inspection began.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"had not swept"},{"id":"B","text":"did not had swept"},{"id":"C","text":"had not sweeping"},{"id":"D","text":"not had swept"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Keep the past-perfect auxiliary and add **not** immediately after it.","explanation":"Place **not after had** and keep **swept**: they had not swept.","keywords":["perfect-negative"],"skill":"perfect-negative","contextKey":"past-perfect-50","fingerprint":"6e10aee21e80b922daaa29c59e97f78c8d502e2a233e5f95de724ad46687ddef","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Keep the past-perfect auxiliary and add **not** immediately after it.","Place **not after had** and keep **swept**: they had not swept.","Choose **had not swept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Place **not after had** and keep **swept**: they had not swept.","B":"Perfect negatives do not add did; not follows the existing auxiliary.","C":"The -ing form needs been for a perfect continuous, which is not requested.","D":"In the standard finite negative, not follows the auxiliary."}}},{"id":"13c20af6-babc-5059-a8fb-19278df90f52","topicSlug":"past-perfect","level":"B1","prompt":"Begin this past-perfect question: ____ they swept the kitchen floor before the inspection began?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Did"},{"id":"B","text":"Are"},{"id":"C","text":"Has"},{"id":"D","text":"Had"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The first auxiliary moves before the subject; do not add a new auxiliary.","explanation":"Move **had** before they. The participle swept remains after the subject.","keywords":["perfect-question"],"skill":"perfect-question","contextKey":"past-perfect-50","fingerprint":"ac99a7a781601e300d1a282f9d5083babd576aeb3f59330bfb1a11147c49d129","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The first auxiliary moves before the subject; do not add a new auxiliary.","Move **had** before they. The participle swept remains after the subject.","Choose **Had**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Did would require a base verb and would not make this perfect question.","B":"Are would create a different structure, not the requested perfect.","C":"Has does not agree with they, and cannot form a past-perfect question.","D":"Move **had** before they. The participle swept remains after the subject."}}},{"id":"18014262-cb9e-5839-ab7a-54e179fbfaeb","topicSlug":"past-perfect","level":"B1","prompt":"Choose the participle for the past perfect: We had ____ the kitchen floor before the inspection began. (sweep)","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"swept"},{"id":"B","text":"sweeping"},{"id":"C","text":"to sweep"},{"id":"D","text":"been sweeping"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Identify the past participle, rather than choosing a word only because it looks past.","explanation":"The past participle of **sweep** is **swept**. It follows had in the requested perfect construction.","keywords":["perfect-participle"],"skill":"perfect-participle","contextKey":"past-perfect-50","fingerprint":"c8968e05808ffca1177ba92734c4aa0c239e0e7cab008fbd4d2cc356ad3fb9ae","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Identify the past participle, rather than choosing a word only because it looks past.","The past participle of **sweep** is **swept**. It follows had in the requested perfect construction.","Choose **swept**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The past participle of **sweep** is **swept**. It follows had in the requested perfect construction.","B":"This is the -ing form, not the past participle.","C":"This is the to-infinitive, not a past participle.","D":"This would make a perfect continuous construction, not perfect simple."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["The past perfect looks backwards from a past reference point. It marks the earlier event with had + past participle. This is useful when the narrative moves out of chronological order. The later event usually uses past simple. Once the order is clear, a story does not need past perfect in every sentence.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Connect two points in time","explanation":"The past perfect looks backwards from a past reference point. It marks the earlier event with had + past participle. This is useful when the narrative moves out of chronological order. The later event usually uses past simple. Once the order is clear, a story does not need past perfect in every sentence.","pattern":"Subject + **had / had + past participle**","commonMistakes":["Before and after can already show the sequence, so past simple is sometimes enough. Past perfect is not required merely because an event happened a long time ago."],"examples":["Use the past perfect: She **had prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began.","Use the past perfect: She **had restored** the old portrait before the inspection began."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Build the perfect verb","explanation":"A perfect construction uses **have as an auxiliary**, followed by a past participle. Regular participles normally end in -ed. Irregular ones must be learned with their base and past forms: write / wrote / written. Do not substitute an -ing form unless you also build the continuous construction.","pattern":"She **had written**. / They **had written**.","commonMistakes":["The form written is a participle; wrote is past simple. They are not interchangeable after have."],"examples":["Make a past-perfect negative: They **had not prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began.","Make a past-perfect negative: They **had not restored** the old portrait before the inspection began."],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Negatives and questions","explanation":"Put **not after had or had**. For a yes/no question, move the auxiliary before the subject. The participle stays unchanged. There is no extra do or did because the perfect construction already contains an auxiliary.","pattern":"Had they finished? / They **had not finished**.","commonMistakes":["Do not combine did with a perfect auxiliary to make its negative or question."],"examples":["Begin this past-perfect question: **Had** they prepared the visitor guide before the inspection began?","Begin this past-perfect question: **Had** they restored the old portrait before the inspection began?"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Time expressions and short answers","explanation":"Read the whole sentence to locate the reference time. Here it is **before the inspection began**. Keep the appropriate form of have in a short answer and omit the repeated participle. Since names a starting point; for names a duration. Neither word automatically selects a single tense.","pattern":"Yes, they **had**. / No, they **had not**.","commonMistakes":["Been can describe a completed visit; gone often means the person has left and has not yet returned."],"examples":["Choose the participle for the past perfect: We had **prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began. (prepare)","Choose the participle for the past perfect: We had **restored** the old portrait before the inspection began. (restore)"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["Subject + **had / had + past participle**","She **had written**. / They **had written**.","Had they finished? / They **had not finished**.","Yes, they **had**. / No, they **had not**."]},"stage":"3 · Time and experience","prerequisites":["present-perfect-continuous"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
